\subsection*{B.1 --- Basic execution (dipolar equilibrium)}

To display the complete list of available command-line parameters and options, run:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --help
\end{verbatim}

Square $8\times8$ array with all defaults (steel needle with automatically computed moment and inertia, no external field):
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 8 --M 8 --geometry square
\end{verbatim}
Generates summary images (initial state, equilibrium, comparison, order parameter) and \code{compass\_field\_log.csv} in the current directory. For the other lattices: \code{--geometry triangular} or \code{--geometry honeycomb}.

ReplacementChunks.TargetContent:
\subsection*{B.1 --- Basic execution (dipolar equilibrium)}

Square $8\times8$ array with all defaults (steel needle with automatically computed moment and inertia, no external field):
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 8 --M 8 --geometry square
\end{verbatim}
Generates summary images (initial state, equilibrium, comparison, order parameter) and \code{compass\_field\_log.csv} in the current directory. For the other lattices: \code{--geometry triangular} or \code{--geometry honeycomb}.

ReplacementChunks.ReplacementContent:
\subsection*{B.3 --- Video and figures in good resolution}

For arrays above $15\times15$, use \code{--dpi} 150--200. Smaller \code{--frame\_every} = smoother animation (more frames). Use \code{--keep\_frames} to retain the folder of individual frame PNGs:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 20 --M 20 --t_sim 4.0 \
    --video demo --dpi 180 --fps 30 --frame_every 3 --keep_frames
\end{verbatim}
The video receives automatic numbering (\code{demo0000.mp4}, \code{demo0001.mp4}, \dots). For static high-resolution figures only (no video), omit \code{--video} and use \code{--dpi 200}.

