150: \item \textbf{Performance instrumentation}: report of integration time, steps per second, and ms/step at the end of each run; progress bar with percentage; explicit \code{--gpu} flag;
151: \item \textbf{Automatic moment of inertia} from the real geometry (\SI{0.4}{mm} steel sheet), replaceable by parameter;
152: \item \textbf{Interruption handling}: \mbox{Ctrl+I} interrupts gracefully (saving video/data); \mbox{Ctrl+C} aborts immediately. The investigation revealed that the default handler was masked by the integration loop, and this was corrected (exit code 130);
153: \item \textbf{Rendering optimization (V35)}: replacement of thousands of individual matplotlib calls by vectorized collections (\emph{PatchCollections} + \emph{quiver}), with a measured speedup of \textbf{17--30$\times$}: a $25\times25$ array that exceeded 30~s of rendering dropped to 3.9~s, and $40\times40$ (previously impractical) dropped to 14~s, with bitwise validation of polygon geometry and physics CSV;
154: \item \textbf{Automatic magnetic moment (V37)} from the saturated volume ($M_s = \SI{1.59e6}{A/m}$, researched for ordinary carbon steel), and correction of a regression where \code{--progress\_bar 0} also suppressed periodic status messages;
155: \item \textbf{Magnetic-domain visualization (V38)}: halo coloring by domain. Neighboring needles whose directions differ by less than an angular tolerance (\code{--domain\_tol}, default $15^\circ$) are grouped through \emph{union--find}; small domains ($<\max(3;\,2\%)$ of the needles) are painted neutral gray so the main domains stand out (Figure~\ref{fig:domains}). This feature began as visualization and became a measurement instrument: the same routine feeds the domain-size statistics of the scientific campaign.
156: \end{itemize}
