245: \begin{figure}[t]
246: \centering
247: \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{figs/tau_vs_Q.png}
248: \caption{Central test of the study (pilot): avalanche power-law exponent $\tau$ as a function of the quality factor $Q$, by geometry. The systematic increase of $\tau$ with $Q$, if confirmed in the full campaign with adequate statistics, is the candidate signature that the universality class depends on the damping regime under dipolar coupling.}
249: \label{fig:tau}
250: \end{figure}
251: 
252: \begin{figure}[t]
253: \centering
254: \includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figs/area_vs_Q.png}
255: \caption{Pilot: hysteresis-loop area as a function of $Q$, by geometry. The area increases monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (more dissipation per cycle), with the three geometries qualitatively similar in this metric, unlike domain size, for which the triangular lattice shows non-monotonic behavior to be investigated.}
256: \label{fig:area}
257: \end{figure}
258: 
259: Two secondary findings from the pilot are worth recording: (i) loop area and coercive field increase monotonically when entering the overdamped regime (Figure~\ref{fig:area}); (ii) the mean domain size in the \emph{triangular} lattice shows non-monotonicity with $Q$ that is not seen in the other geometries, exactly the kind of frustration~$\times$~inertia interaction identified in the study design as a type-B candidate result. Both require the full campaign for confirmation.
260: 
261: \subsection{Full campaign (ready to run)}
262: 
263: Defined parameters: $30\times30$ lattice (900 needles), 8 log-spaced $Q$ values in $[0.05;\,15]$, 5 seeds, 3 geometries $\Rightarrow$ 120 hysteresis runs + 120 relaxation runs. Cost with V39 code: $\sim$2--2.5~min per run ($\approx 7$--8~h serial campaign). \textbf{With V40 optimization} (precomputed dipolar tensor, Section~\ref{sec:improvements}): $\sim$3.3--3.4~s per run $\Rightarrow$ \textbf{$\sim$15~min serial campaign on a single core}, and only a few minutes with \code{--n\_workers} on the \code{jps@mumax} server, with no GPU required.
