\noindent\textbf{(6) step\_neg} --- field from $t{=}0$, removed at \code{field\_delay}, then kept at zero until the end:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 15 --M 15 --field_mode step_neg \
    --field_delay 0.3 --B_ext 0.003 --t_sim 3.0
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{(7) forc} --- First-Order Reversal Curve. The system starts at positive saturation $B_{\max}$, ramps down to a reversal field $B_r$ (\code{--forc\_Br}), and sweeps back to $B_{\max}$ to trace a minor loop. The duration of saturation and reversal phases can be controlled via \code{--forc\_t\_sat} and \code{--forc\_t\_rev}. The curve is exported to \code{forc\_curve.csv} and can also be visualized as a dual-panel video showing the minor loop being traced in real time:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 20 --M 20 --field_mode forc \
    --ext_Bx 0.005 --forc_Br -0.002 --t_sim 4.0 --video forc --dpi 160
\end{verbatim}

\noindent In \code{hysteresis}, the $M(B)$ loop is exported to \code{hysteresis\_loop.csv}; in \code{forc}, to \code{forc\_curve.csv}; in \code{sine}, to \code{sine\_field.csv}. In pulse/step modes, \code{--torque\_tol} controls the equilibrium criterion and \code{--t\_sim\_full 1} disables early stopping (Appendix~A).

