\noindent\textbf{(7) forc} --- First-Order Reversal Curve (FORC) suite. The system simulates a complete family of first-order reversal curves. Each cycle saturates the array at $+B_{\max}$, ramps down to a reversal field $B_{r, k}$ (spaced evenly from $+B_{\max}$ to the minimum reversal field \code{--forc\_Br\_min}), and sweeps back to $+B_{\max}$. The sweep is repeated for a total of \code{--forc\_n\_curves} cycles. Timing is automatically calculated so that the total time is $t_{\mathrm{sim}} = N_{\mathrm{curves}} \cdot (t_{\mathrm{sat}} + t_{\mathrm{ramp\_down}} + t_{\mathrm{ramp\_up}})$. The entire suite of curves is logged to \code{forc\_curve.csv} containing the reversal field $B_r$ as a fifth column:
\begin{verbatim}
# Run a family of 30 FORC curves, sweeping Br down to -5 mT
python3 compass.py --N 20 --M 20 --field_mode forc \
    --ext_Bx 0.005 --forc_Br_min -0.005 --forc_n_curves 30
\end{verbatim}

ReplacementChunks.TargetContent:
\noindent\textbf{(7) forc} --- First-Order Reversal Curve. The system starts at positive saturation $B_{\max}$, ramps down to a reversal field $B_r$ (\code{--forc\_Br}), and sweeps back to $B_{\max}$ to trace a minor loop. The duration of saturation and reversal phases can be controlled via \code{--forc\_t\_sat} and \code{--forc\_t\_rev}. The curve is exported to \code{forc\_curve.csv} and can also be visualized as a dual-panel video showing the minor loop being traced in real time:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 compass.py --N 20 --M 20 --field_mode forc \
    --ext_Bx 0.005 --forc_Br -0.002 --t_sim 4.0 --video forc --dpi 160
\end{verbatim}

